%I A395689 #21 Aug 16 2026 22:50:09
%S A395689 1,0,1,2,48,10752,49390080,4084683374592
%N A395689 Number of idempotent Latin squares of order n.
%C A395689 A Latin square of order n on the symbols 0..n-1 is idempotent if the cell in row i, column i contains i for every i; that is, the main diagonal reads 0, 1, ..., n-1.
%C A395689 Equivalently, a(n) is the number of idempotent quasigroups on n elements: Cayley tables of binary operations satisfying x*x = x in which every left translation and every right translation is a bijection.
%C A395689 Squares are counted individually. These are labeled counts, with no reduction by any symmetry.
%C A395689 a(2) = 0 because neither of the two 2 X 2 Latin squares has both 0 and 1 on its main diagonal.
%C A395689 The first row of an idempotent Latin square fixes the symbol 0 and moves every other symbol, since symbol j already occupies the diagonal cell in column j. Its restriction to the remaining n-1 columns is therefore a derangement, so there are A000166(n-1) possible first rows.
%e A395689 The unique idempotent Latin square of order 3 (so a(3) = 1):
%e A395689   0 2 1
%e A395689   2 1 0
%e A395689   1 0 2
%o A395689 (Python)
%o A395689 def A395689(n):
%o A395689     L = [[i if i == j else -1 for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]
%o A395689     def count(k):
%o A395689         if k == n * n:
%o A395689             return 1
%o A395689         i, j = divmod(k, n)
%o A395689         if L[i][j] != -1:
%o A395689             return count(k + 1)
%o A395689         t = 0
%o A395689         for s in range(n):
%o A395689             if s not in L[i] and all(L[r][j] != s for r in range(n)):
%o A395689                 L[i][j] = s
%o A395689                 t += count(k + 1)
%o A395689                 L[i][j] = -1
%o A395689         return t
%o A395689     return count(0)
%o A395689 print([A395689(n) for n in range(1, 7)])
%Y A395689 Cf. A000166, A002860, A395690, A398445.
%K A395689 nonn,new
%O A395689 1,4
